@using Odyssey.Client.Authorization
@using Odyssey.Client.Components
@using Odyssey.Dtos.Authorization
@using Odyssey.Dtos.Finance
@attribute [Authorize(Policy = PermissionClaims.ContractsUpdate)]
@inject IContractsApiClient Contracts
@inject ISnackbar Snackbar

@*
    AddContractEventDialog — add OR edit ONE event on a contract (issue #138: POST …/events and
    PUT …/events/{eventId}). Mirrors the design system's AddContractEventModal.

    Four rules the dialog has to carry, each one a server rule a user would otherwise meet as an
    error — or, in the third case, never meet at all, which is worse:

    1. THREE FREE-TEXT FIELDS, THREE ROLES (§4.1). Title is required for EVERY type — a predetermined
       type does not derive it and does not make it optional, `Other` is the catch-all member, and the
       user's own wording is what the log reads back. Description is the longer account and appears on
       the rail beside the title. Notes are the user's own working notes and are the one field the
       rail does not show.

    2. OCCURRED AT IS A DATE AND A TIME, and must not be in the future (§8.3). The bound is the server
       clock with a 60-second forward tolerance, so the field refuses "next Tuesday" and accepts "now"
       from a slightly fast clock. Planned events are a non-goal: this is a record of what happened.

    3. "NOT ON THE TIMELINE" IS NOT "PRIVATE", AND THE LABEL MUST NOT SAY IT IS. Notes sit behind the
       same contracts.read claim as everything else, are searched by the same term and are exported
       with the rest — and contracts.read is held by Admin, Owner and User, so in a household install
       the audience is everyone but Guest. The spec forbids "Private note" or any phrasing implying a
       narrower audience, because a user who believes the field is private will put things in it that
       the label promised to protect. The helper text states the audience outright.

    4. THE PUT IS A FULL REPLACEMENT (§5.3), the opposite of UpdateContract. An omitted description or
       note clears it; an omitted type resets to Other. The dialog says so where the decision is made.

    No links: the four contact / account columns were removed in draft v5 (Non-Goal 5), so there is
    nothing to pick here and no delete anywhere else to guard.
*@

<OdsModal Open="Open" OpenChanged="OpenChanged" OnClose="CloseAsync"
          Icon="history" Title="@TitleFragment" Subtitle="@SubtitleFragment" Class="con-dialog">
    <ChildContent>
        <div class="ods-modal-body">

            @* A plain registry select, not a card grid: eighteen members is still a list, and the type
               CLASSIFIES rather than being the thing the reader scans for — the title is.

               `source` is NOT a field here (issue #154 §7.4) — not as a control, not as a disabled
               one. A disabled input would imply it is ordinarily settable, when in fact it is absent
               from both write DTOs and cannot be set at all. The row says so in its attribution line
               on the rail, and this dialog says so in the Title field's help text.

               OdsTypeSelect, not the generic OdsSelect: it draws each member in its own glyph and
               category colour straight from the registry, and carries the listbox semantics, keyboard
               model and error association rather than leaving them to be re-derived here. There is no
               OdsContractEventTypeSelect wrapper because the design system defines no
               ContractEventTypeSelect either — its own dialog uses the generic select. *@
            <OdsTypeSelect Id="cev-type" Label="Type" Placeholder="Select type…"
                           Value="@_type.ToString()" ValueChanged="PickType"
                           Types="OdsTypeRegistries.ContractEventTypes" Help="@TypeHelp" />

            @* The helper and the error ride on the DATE PICKER, not on the shell. OdsFieldShell derives
               its help/error element ids from HtmlFor, and a two-control group has no single control
               to point HtmlFor at — so a shell-level Help or Error here would render text with no id
               and nothing referencing it, leaving the group undescribed (WCAG 1.3.1). MudBlazor wires
               its own aria-describedby for HelperText/ErrorText, and the date is the control the
               future bound is actually about, so the message lands where the fix is. *@
            <OdsFieldShell Label="When" Required="true" LabelId="cev-when-label">
                <div class="cev-when" role="group" aria-labelledby="cev-when-label">
                    <OdsDatePicker Value="_date" ValueChanged="OnDateChanged"
                                   Label="Date" Required="true" Max="Today"
                                   Placeholder="Pick a date"
                                   Error="@_errors.ContainsKey("occurredAt")"
                                   ErrorText="@_errors.GetValueOrDefault("occurredAt")"
                                   HelperText="@(_errors.ContainsKey("occurredAt") ? null : "Date and time, in the past. A log, not a plan.")" />
                    <OdsTimeField Label="Time" Value="_time" ValueChanged="OnTimeChanged" />
                </div>
            </OdsFieldShell>

            @* Required for EVERY type, `Other` included — the type classifies, the title is what the
               reader actually reads. *@
            <OdsField Id="cev-title" Label="Title" Required="true" AutoFocus="true"
                      Value="@_title" ValueChanged="OnTitleChanged"
                      MaxLength="256" Error="@_errors.GetValueOrDefault("title")"
                      Placeholder="e.g. Emailed landlord about the rent increase"
                      Help="@(_errors.ContainsKey("title") ? null : SystemTitleHelp ?? "A short label. This is the line the timeline shows.")" />

            <OdsNoteField Id="cev-description" Label="Description" MaxLength="1024"
                          Value="@_description" ValueChanged="@(v => _description = v)"
                          Placeholder="What happened, at more length — what was said, what was agreed…"
                          Help="Shown on the timeline under the title." />

            @* The audience is stated, never implied to be narrower (§4.1). *@
            <OdsNoteField Id="cev-notes" Label="Notes" MaxLength="1024"
                          Value="@_notes" ValueChanged="@(v => _notes = v)"
                          Placeholder="Reminders to yourself — what to chase, what to check next time…"
                          Help="Your working notes. Kept off the timeline, but anyone who can see this contract can read them." />

            @if (IsEdit)
            {
                <OdsAlert Severity="Severity.Info">
                    Saving replaces the whole event — type, title, description, notes and date. Text you
                    clear is cleared on the contract, and a type you leave unset resets to
                    <strong>Other</strong>. Who recorded this event, and when, is never rewritten.
                    @* The full replacement bites hardest on a recorded row: its description was
                       written once, at the moment of the change, and no later write re-derives it. *@
                    @if (IsSystemEvent)
                    {
                        <text> Nothing regenerates a description you clear on a recorded event.</text>
                    }
                </OdsAlert>
            }
        </div>
    </ChildContent>
    <Footer>
        <OdsButton Variant="OdsButtonVariant.Text" OnClick="CloseAsync">Cancel</OdsButton>
        <OdsButton Variant="OdsButtonVariant.Filled" Icon="@(IsEdit ? "check" : "add")"
                   Loading="_isSaving" OnClick="SubmitAsync">
            @(IsEdit ? "Save changes" : "Create event")
        </OdsButton>
    </Footer>
</OdsModal>

@code {
    // The title and subtitle are inline render fragments — Razor syntax, so they cannot live in the
    // code-behind. Everything else does.
    private RenderFragment TitleFragment => @<text>@(IsEdit ? "Edit event" : "New event")</text>;

    @* Repeated from the Title field's help text, for sighted users. The help text is what carries it
       programmatically — this line is not wired to anything and must never be the only place the
       fact appears. *@
    private RenderFragment SubtitleFragment => IsEdit
        ? IsSystemEvent
            ? @<text>Recorded automatically. Changing it changes this log line, not the contract.</text>
            : @<text>Change what this entry records. Who recorded it, and when, does not change.</text>
        : @<text>Record something that has happened to @Contract.Name.</text>;
}
